coming in on this and working with  Ridley what tell us about first of all  how you got to the project and a little  bit about the sort of the nature of that  collaboration I had been working on lost  for six years and during that time  television is an all consuming job we  did 121 hours of that show in over the  course of six years and the only other  movie project that i did was i produced  the first Star Trek film which was great  because we kind of did a TV style and JJ  myself Alex Kurtzman and Bob horsey who  wrote the script all sort of  collaborated between the second and  third seasons of lost and then the  writer strike happened right around the  time that we went into production so I  was able to be on that set and was  really proud of that film but i but but  but didn't write it and then a CAA kept  calling and saying like we have you know  this great opportunity and I was like I  just I don't even want to know about it  don't tell me what it is so lost ended  in may of 2010 and then I went to Italy  to reintroduce myself to my family who I  hadn't seen they turned it they were  doing well we had a lot of catching up  to do and so we we were in Europe for a  month and then I came back and um and I  got a phone call I was driving on  Ventura Boulevard and studio city I just  had a very nice lunch at arts  delicatessen and my my agent called me  and said Ridley Scott's going to call  you in five minutes you available and I  slammed on the brakes of my car which is  what you do when you get that call and  and I pulled over and five minutes later  Ridley called me and I just answered my  phone and there was no pomp or  circumstance there was no assistance  saying I have at least got for you I he  was just like hey dude I'm sending you a  script and I was like if you actually do  calls me dude still I'm not entirely  sure he knows my name and and at the  time I thought it I was like this is a  case of mistaken identity he thinks out  of someone else like David Benioff or  something but I'm just going to roll  with it and I said I yeah absolutely  i'll read anything that you send me so i  called my agent back and I said  you know what this isn't he says I have  no idea so about two hours later a guy  showed up in my house and he handed me a  screenplay and he said I'll be waiting  in my car in front of your house and  when you're finished you can hand it  back to me and right then I was like  this is the alien thing that I've been  hearing about and uh so I'm sure you are  a geek like me We we'd been tracking  that the fact that Ridley was going to  direct an alien prequel for a couple of  years it was on my radar when I got that  screenplay and sure enough the script  was written by John Speights who I share  writing credit with on the movie I've  befriended over the process I thought  the script was great but it was a very  um dyed-in-the-wool prequel so there's  eggs there's facehuggers there's chest  bursters there Xena Morris with acid for  blood by page like 35 like that's what  it is it just happens to take place  before alien but there was this other  amazing original idea in the movie that  I thought that basically sort of gave a  thematic weight to the franchise which  had traditionally either been a horror  movie or an action movie or god knows  what after that and so the idea that all  of this sort of got kick-started by  mankind's desire to sort of figure out  what its origins were I was like oh  here's another question that I don't  ever have to answer it drew me and  immediately and but there was a real  thumb at equate to it and I don't know  how I got an Ridley's radar but I sent  out an email that night basically saying  I think that you can strip away a lot of  this stuff that is alienate there and  just sort of changed the balance of the  script so that it's not seventy percent  alien thirty percent new idea and flip  it so it's 7030 the other way and you  know if you if you if you'll have me  would 